{% set webhookRoute = "/spectrum/webhook" %}
{% set spectrumInstance = symbol("ts:spectrum-ts#SpectrumInstance") %}

[Convex](https://convex.dev) is a reactive backend whose functions are
request-scoped, so it cannot host the long-lived `for await (… of app.messages)`
loop that a {{ spectrumInstance | typeRef }} normally runs.

Webhooks are the usual answer for that — but `app.webhook()` dispatches its
handler **after** the HTTP response, and on Convex the isolate is frozen the
moment that response is returned. The handler would never finish.

The [`@spectrum-ts/convex`](https://github.com/photon-hq/spectrum-convex)
component inverts the order: a delivery is verified and written to a table
*before* the `200` is sent, so an acknowledged message is already durable.
Everything after that runs on Convex's scheduler.

It also ships the pipeline from [Architecture](/best-practices/architecture) and
[Inbound pipeline](/best-practices/inbound-pipeline) — debouncing, cancellation,
carry-forward, paced sends — as component tables and scheduled functions, so
you don't rebuild it per project. None of it is imposed: by default every
message is simply handled on its own.

## Install

```bash
npm i @spectrum-ts/convex
```

```typescript
// convex/convex.config.ts
import { defineApp } from "convex/server";
import spectrum from "@spectrum-ts/convex/convex.config";

const app = defineApp();
app.use(spectrum);
export default app;
```

Set three environment variables in the Convex dashboard:

| Variable | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `SPECTRUM_WEBHOOK_SECRET` | Verifies each delivery's HMAC |
| `SPECTRUM_PROJECT_ID` | Spectrum Cloud project |
| `SPECTRUM_PROJECT_SECRET` | Spectrum Cloud secret |

## Wire it up

```typescript
// convex/spectrum.ts
import { Spectrum } from "@spectrum-ts/convex";
import { components, internal } from "./_generated/api";

export const spectrum = new Spectrum(components.spectrum, {
  onBatch: internal.agent.respond,
  sender: internal.sender.deliver,
  pacingMs: 600,
});
```

```typescript
// convex/http.ts
import { httpRouter } from "convex/server";
import { spectrum } from "./spectrum";

const http = httpRouter();
spectrum.registerRoutes(http);
export default http;
```

Point your Spectrum Cloud webhook at
`https://<deployment>.convex.site{{ webhookRoute }}`.

## Handle a turn

`onBatch` is invoked once per settled burst, not once per message.

```typescript
// convex/agent.ts
export const respond = internalAction({
  args: {
    spaceId: v.string(),
    chainId: v.string(),
    messages: v.array(v.any()),
    carried: v.array(v.any()),
  },
  handler: async (ctx, args) => {
    const reply = await generate(args.messages, args.carried);

    // `ctx.scheduler.cancel` cannot stop an action that is already running, so
    // re-check after anything slow and bail out if a newer message arrived.
    if (await spectrum.isCancelled(ctx, args)) {
      return null;
    }

    await spectrum.send(ctx, {
      spaceId: args.spaceId,
      chainId: args.chainId,
      content: { type: "text", text: reply },
    });
    await spectrum.completeChain(ctx, args);
    return null;
  },
});
```

`carried` holds messages an earlier, cancelled turn had already drained. They
are older than `messages`, so present them as prior context (`[Earlier message]
…`) rather than as part of the current question.

<Note>
Native webhook deliveries are at-least-once. The component dedupes on the
provider message id before anything else runs, so a redelivery neither stores a
second row nor restarts the debounce window.
</Note>

## Sending

Sends go through a durable outbox. Each row carries a `clientGuid` derived from
`(chainId, seq)`, so the same logical send is never queued twice.

<Warning>
The guid is **not** forwarded to the provider. Spectrum's send path does not
accept a caller-supplied `clientMessageId`, and the SDK mints a fresh
idempotency key per call, so a retry after a lost acknowledgement can still
post the message twice.
</Warning>

The transport itself lives in an action **your app** owns:

```typescript
// convex/sender.ts
"use node";

import { createCloudSender } from "@spectrum-ts/convex/sender";

const sender = createCloudSender();
```

<Warning>
Outbound sends need two pieces of setup, and fail without them.

Install the provider and the gRPC peers — `@spectrum-ts/imessage` is an
*optional* peer, so it is not installed for you:

```bash
npm i @spectrum-ts/imessage @photon-ai/advanced-imessage nice-grpc nice-grpc-common @grpc/grpc-js
```

Then mark them external in `convex.json` **and** add a file that re-exports the
three peers:

```typescript
// convex/_workaround.ts — nothing imports this; it exists so the packages ship
"use node";
export * as _grpcJs from "@grpc/grpc-js";
export * as _niceGrpc from "nice-grpc";
export * as _niceGrpcCommon from "nice-grpc-common";
```

Convex installs an `externalPackages` entry only if the bundled code imports it.
These three are optional peers loaded dynamically from inside
`@photon-ai/advanced-imessage`, so nothing references them and they are never
installed — `createGrpcClient` then reports them as missing even though they are
in your `package.json`.

gRPC is the transport for both directions: the SDK's HTTP client has no
`subscribeEvents` or `EventsResource`, so it cannot serve inbound.
</Warning>

Build the sender once per module rather than per send, so warm containers skip
the token mint and channel handshake. Note that `node.externalPackages` is not
honoured by the anonymous local backend, so test sends against a cloud
deployment.

Inbound attachments work the same way: the webhook delivers metadata only, and
`fetchAttachment()` pulls the bytes over gRPC from a Node action. Store them
with `ctx.storage.store()` — Convex documents cap at 1 MiB, so bytes belong in
file storage.

## What you get

- **You choose how messages become turns.** `mode: "immediate"` (the default)
  handles every message on its own — N messages, N replies. `mode: "collapse"`
  makes a burst one turn: a follow-up supersedes the in-flight turn and cancels
  any reply that has not gone out, so four messages in eight seconds produce a
  single answer. `debounceMs` adds a settling window on top of collapse mode,
  at the cost of latency on every reply.
- **Nothing is dropped on cancellation.** Messages stay queued until a flush
  drains them; anything drained but unanswered is carried into the next batch.
- **A superseded turn cannot talk over its replacement.** Liveness is decided by
  chain identity rather than by comparing timestamps, so it stays correct even
  when two pipeline events land in the same millisecond.
